  "full_response": "In operating systems, **processes** and **threads** are both mechanisms for executing programs, but they differ in key ways:\n\n### **1. Definition**\n- **Process**:\n  - A **process** is an instance of a program in execution.\n  - It includes the program code, data, resources (like file handles, memory), and execution state.\n  - Each process runs in its own **isolated memory space** (protected by the OS).\n\n- **Thread**:\n  - A **thread** is the smallest unit of execution within a process.\n  - Multiple threads can exist within a single process, sharing the same memory and resources.\n  - Threads are sometimes called **\"lightweight processes\"** because they have lower overhead than processes.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Key Differences**\n\n| Feature            | Process                          | Thread                          |\n|--------------------|----------------------------------|---------------------------------|\n| **Memory Space**   | Separate (isolated)              | Shared within the same process  |\n| **Creation Time**  | Slower (requires OS intervention)| Faster (managed within process) |\n| **Overhead**       | High (context switching is costly)| Low (minimal overhead)         |\n| **Communication**  | IPC (Inter-Process Communication) | Direct (shared memory)         |\n| **Independence**   | Independent                      | Dependent (if process crashes, all threads die) |\n| **Resource Usage** | More (separate memory, files)    | Less (shares resources)        |\n| **Synchronization**| Not needed (isolated)            | Needed (race conditions possible) |\n\n---\n\n### **3. When to Use Each?**\n- **Use Processes When:**\n  - You need **strong isolation** (e.g., running untrusted code).\n  - You want **fault tolerance** (one process crashing doesn’t affect others).\n  - You need **parallelism across CPU cores** (though threads can also do this).\n\n- **Use Threads When:**\n  - You need **faster communication** (shared memory is quicker than IPC).\n  - You want **lower overhead** (thread creation is cheaper than process creation).\n  - You need **concurrent execution** (e.g., handling multiple client requests in a server).\n\n---\n\n### **4. Example**\n- **Process Example:**\n  - Running **Chrome browser** (each tab may run as a separate process for stability).\n  - Running **Microsoft Word** and **Excel** simultaneously",
